      "writtenOn": "2019-06-14T17:55:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "discussed offline today. converging on:\n\n1) do something like this to force the compiler to not optimize out the copy to the intermediate aligned location\nstatic void cp8w(size_t offset, const p256_int *src)\n{\n\tint i;\n        uint32_t word __attribute__((aligned(4)));  /* and/or set volatile? */\n        uint32_t *dst \u003d ... + base_offset;\n\n        for (i \u003d 0; i \u003c P256_NDIGITS; i++, dst++) {\n                word \u003d P256_DIGIT(src, i);\n                *dst \u003d word;\n        }\n}\n\nanother alt is to turn P256_DIGIT into a function returning uint32_t and somehow prevent the compiler from inlining it, so that we actually get an aligned uint32_t on stack and the compiler can use word-copy to *dst.\n\n2) this is probably sufficient to guarantee word-copies, but check with compiler guys if there\u0027s anything in the standard and what are the practical guarantees. we are probably not the first interested in this.",
      "parentUuid": "767ff973_0d4136cd",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 769,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 772,
        "endChar": 30
      },
      "revId": "78d98b5dc7c3b2a73335b46f9fedfd4d08a88c16",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6c28e3f9_e634f812",
        "filename": "chip/g/dcrypto/dcrypto_p256.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 772,
      "author": {
        "id": 1152319
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-07-04T00:10:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I looked a bit more at the problem, and I\u0027m convinced that the only issue was that we were copying from to p256_int* in cp8w, p256_add_d and similar functions in the past: while the functions were not inlined they only could rely on being passed an arbitrary p256_int* and used str.b. As soon as LTO led to inlining those, it saw that it was actually copying to an always aligned address and fixed the generated code. I suspect, it was actually inlining for cp8w that fixed the behavior. \n\nI.e. as long as the compiler sees that it copies to an aligned unit32_t*, we shall be ok.\nAlthough, I couldn\u0027t find a concrete guarantee that llvm always generates str.w in case of seeing \"store i32 %word, i32* %dst align 4\", places like this [https://llvm.org/docs/Frontend/PerformanceTips.html#when-to-specify-alignment] came close enough to confirming that, so I personally am convinced str.w is guaranteed w/o writing an explicit assembly when the destination is aligned.\n\nAnd if so, having \nstatic void cp1w(...) {\n        uint32_t *dst \u003d ...;\n\n        for (i \u003d 0; i \u003c P256_NDIGITS; i++, dst++) {\n                *dst \u003d P256_DIGIT(src, i);\n        }\n}\nshould be enough in the new approach where we switch to offsets instead of p256_int* pointers.\nShall we do just that? Unf, I couldn\u0027t find a way to enforce aligned accesses for a specific type or structure w/o global -mno-unaligned-access, which we can\u0027t set.\n\nAn alt approach is using __aeabi_memcpy4, in place of hand-written assembly, which should also do the right thing if clang properly supports it [http://infocenter.arm.com/help/topic/com.arm.doc.faqs/ka3934.html]. \n    uint32_t word \u003d P256_DIGIT(src, i);\n    __aeabi_memcpy4(dst, \u0026word, 4);\nThis may force the creation of aligned |word| that can\u0027t be optimized out since we need its address.",
      "parentUuid": "d1281410_39eb11df",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 769,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 772,
        "endChar": 30
      },
